<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="no"?>
<!--
    Feed profiles and the real-equivalent nutrition mode (scripts/feed).

    Off by default: the mode is chosen per savegame in the RL settings (Nutrition model: Legacy /
    Real-equivalent). It runs only for cattle (animal type COW) under the weight model v2
    (weightProfiles.xml), with the body condition on and this file valid. In a pen it runs only
    when every animal in it is covered and no calf there is still on milk outside a calf rearing
    pen; any other pen keeps the old rules as a whole, and its info says why.

    Real nutrition, game resources. An animal's intake and needs are worked out in real units -
    kg of dry matter (DM) a real day, ME in MJ - and only the feed taken from the trough is scaled:

        real days an hour stands for   dtDays = realDaysPerPeriod / (24 x days per period)
        liters taken for kg DM         L = resourceScale x kgDM / (density x dm)
        kg DM a liter eaten restores   kgDM = L x density x dm / resourceScale

    resourceScale appears once on each side and nowhere else: weight, growth, health and milk do
    not change with it, only how much game feed the same nutrition costs (default 0.125: one eighth
    of the real-equivalent volume, not of legacy consumption).
    It is a game resource discount, not a five-fold better feed conversion. Water, straw, care,
    pen capacity, prices and output are not touched. One game month is realDaysPerPeriod model days
    (a convention: seasons stay as they are).

    Root
    version             Feed data version (saved with each pen's monthly record).
    resourceScale       Game liters per real-equivalent liter (0 < s <= 1).
    realDaysPerPeriod   Model days in a game month.
    stepDays            Model days of one feeding and growth step. A pen keeps a model clock; each
                        game hour adds its model days and the pen runs a step for each whole
                        stepDays it holds (5 in an hour of a one-day month, one every 5-6 hours of
                        a 28-day month), so any month length runs the same steps. What is left of
                        the clock is saved with the pen.

    requirements (ME, MJ; per real day unless noted)
    maintenanceMEPerKgMetabolic  ME for maintenance per kg^0.75 of live weight (x maintenance gene).
                                 NRC 2000: NEm 0.077 Mcal/kg^0.75 = 0.322 MJ, km about 0.64.
    growthMEPerKgAt500           ME per kg of frame grown at 500 kg live weight; it scales with
    growthWeightExponent         (weight / 500) ^ this (the NRC 2000 NEg equation at about 1 kg a
    growthMEPerKgMin             day, kg about 0.42), never below the minimum (NRC 2001 calf).
    reserveDepositMEPerKg        ME per kg of body reserve laid down (about 31 MJ retained / kf 0.6).
    reserveMobilizeMEPerKg       ME a kg of reserve gives back (31 MJ x 0.8 / km 0.65). Below the
                                 deposit cost, so the reserve can never be cycled into energy.
    catabolismMEPerKg            ME a kg of frame gives back once the reserve is spent.
    pregnancyFactor              Extra ME per fetus at term, as a share of maintenance.

    calf (a calf on milk, NRC 2001 "Nutrient requirements of the young calf"; ME and NE in MJ / Mcal)
    maintenanceMEPerKgMetabolic  ME for maintenance of a milk-fed calf per kg^0.75 (0.1 Mcal ME =
                                 NEm 0.086 / km 0.86). It moves to the adult value with the calf's milk
                                 cap (milkMax, calfRearing.xml): weaned, the adult value exactly.
    negCoefficient,              The energy in its gain: NEg (Mcal a day) = negCoefficient x
    negWeightExponent,           W^negWeightExponent x LWG^negGainExponent, LWG the gain of its own
    negGainExponent              curve around its growth phase (at least minGainKgPerDay), so a kg of
    minGainKgPerDay              gain holds NEg / LWG.
    kgMilk, kgSolid              Efficiency of ME for gain from milk (0.69) and from dry feed (0.57); a
                                 calf's moves from the first to the second with its milkMax, and its
                                 cost a kg of gain into the adult cost (requirements) the same way, so
                                 a weaned calf - and every animal from 8 months - pays the adult cost.
                                 About 20 MJ ME a kg at birth, 25 at two months: above the adult floor
                                 (growthMEPerKgMin), as NRC's calf values are.
    The plan and the step's settlement use the same values (RLFeed.getGrowthCost,
    RLFeed.getCalfMaintenanceFactor).

    intake (kg DM per real day)
    growingPerKgMetabolic        Most a growing or finishing animal eats, per kg^0.75 (NRC 2000).
    dryPerKgMetabolic            The same for a cow that has calved and is dry.
    lactatingPerKgMetabolic,     A lactating cow: this x kg^0.75 + lactatingPerKgMilk x real milk
    lactatingPerKgMilk           (kg a day) (NRC 2001).
    calfSolidPerKgMetabolic      Solid feed a calf on milk's rumen takes at full solidMax, per
                                 kg^0.75; x its solidMax (calfRearing.xml), moving to the growing
                                 value from one month before weaning to one month after. Its milk
                                 is its allowance (calfMilk), apart from this; milk and solids
                                 together stay within the growing value.
    ndfFill*                     NDF an animal can hold, as a share of live weight (Mertens: 1.2%):
                                 a bulky ration fills it before the animal's appetite is met.
    All intakes x the maintenance gene's factor (weightProfiles.xml maintenanceGeneEffect).

    health
    shortageBufferDays       Model days of maintenance shortfall the frame may cover before health
                             falls (an empty trough for a few hours is a gap, not a famine); from
                             below the reserve's easy range (bodyCondition.xml minReserve +
                             mobilizeEaseRange) health falls at once. A fully fed day takes a day off.
    extremeUnderweightRatio  Under this share of its curve at its age an animal loses health for its
                             weight alone (the protection against a very stunted animal). Growth
                             that lags its calendar is otherwise only shown, not punished: health
                             follows what the hour's feed and reserve paid for (RLFeedMath).

    protein: crude protein (share of DM) a ration needs for full growth or milk, by stage (NRC 2000
    growing-finishing cattle, NRC 2001 dairy and calves): calf on milk, young (weaned, under
    youngUntilMonth), growing (under growingUntilMonth), finishing, lactating, dry. A lactating cow
    moves from the dry value to the lactating one with her real milk against lactatingAtKg (kg a
    day, NRC 2001: about 16% CP for 30 kg); a calf moves
    from the young value to the calf value with the share of milk it may still drink. Below it, growth
    and milk potential fall linearly to nothing at the maintenance level. A simplified limit, not a
    metabolisable protein model; energy it leaves unused is laid down or lost in the budget.

    ration
    wheatMaxShare         Most wheat in a ration's DM (OSU 2025: about a third, with enough roughage).
    adaptationDays        Model days a rumen needs to go from no concentrate to adaptationMaxShare.
    adaptationMaxShare    Concentrate share a fully adapted animal is adapted to.
    stepAllowance         Concentrate share an animal may eat above what it is adapted to.
    unadaptedUtilisation  What concentrate above the adapted share is worth.
    class                 Per ration class (CALF, GROWING, LACTATING, DRY): peNdfMin, the least
                          physically effective NDF of the ration, and fermentableMax, the most
                          starch + sugar. Concentrate is held below what keeps both.

    milk: the bridge from the game's milk to real milk. A breed's real lactation average (kg a day)
    over its game milk curve's mature value x the lactation mean (bodyCondition.xml) gives real kg
    per game liter; meMJPerKg the ME a real kg needs (NRC 2001: NEL 3.1 MJ/kg at 4% fat / kl 0.62).
    The game's milk output and price are not changed. Breed names are weightProfiles.xml profiles.

    calfMilk: separate intake ceiling, in prepared standard milk (not powder). realLitersPerDay
    is 10 L/day for a reference Holstein, density 1.03 kg/L and dm 0.125 define that reference
    liquid. Scale its DM allowance by breed/sex mean birth weight relative to a Holstein of the
    same sex to sizeExponent (0.75: feeding guides give a Jersey about three quarters of a
    Holstein's milk, more than her weight share) and by calfRearing milkMax, once. Total
    appetite can further reduce it. No extra gene
    multiplier and no growth in the ceiling with current live weight. At default scale 0.125 and 30 days,
    the reference ceiling is 37.5 game L/month before weaning reduction, not guaranteed intake.
    This is a game calibration (8-10 real L/day), not a biological maximum; milk energy and
    protein are still the feed's fixed values, and shortages are never repriced.

    damMilk: a dam's own milk as her suckling calf drinks it (whole milk, NRC 2001: 12.5% DM, ME
    5.37 Mcal = 22.5 MJ/kg DM, CP 25% of DM). In a pen that is not a calf rearing pen a calf on
    milk drinks from its dam: the milk her budget paid this step (game liters, through the milk
    bridge to real kg) goes to her calves up to their allowance - twins share it - and only the
    rest to the pen's milk store; milk in the trough makes up what she did not give, within the
    same allowance. Her milk is never valued twice and never made up from nothing: a calf without
    its lactating dam in the pen keeps the pen on the old rules (SUCKLING_CALF).

    feed: one per fill type.
    role         COMPLETE (a mixed ration, counted once as it is), FORAGE, CONCENTRATE or MILK.
    status       TYPICAL (literature typical value), PROXY (a stated stand-in), UNCONFIGURED (no
                 data: never eaten in the real-equivalent mode, shown as such).
    form         Processing state (text).
    density      kg as fed per game liter. The engine's massPerLiter, a game conversion, not a
                 measured bulk density; used only here, never for vehicles or physics.
    dm           Dry matter share of the fed weight.
    tdn / me     Energy. meBasis TDN_NRC2000: ME (MJ/kg DM) = TDN% x 0.04409 x 0.82 x 4.184
                 (NRC 1996/2000: DE = 0.04409 TDN Mcal/kg, ME = 0.82 DE). ME_DIRECT: me as given.
                 NEm/NEg are never read as ME.
    cp, ndf      Crude protein and NDF, share of DM.
    peFactor     Physically effective share of the NDF.
    starch, sugar  Share of DM (starch + sugar: fermentable).
    stages       Feeding stages it may be fed in (PRE_WEANING WEANING WEANED; calfRearing.xml).
                 A calf on milk eats concentrate its stage allows as a starter, with the forage
                 (CALF rules: fermentableMax), at the feed's own ME; the calf's solidEnergy
                 (rumen development) discounts forage and complete rations only.
    maxShare     Most of a ration's DM the feed may make (optional; wheat: the ration's
                 wheatMaxShare); the concentrate share is held there (limit FEED_CAP; WHEAT for wheat).
    capGroup     Feeds sharing one cap: together at most the cap (grain grist counts with wheat).
    source, year Evidence.

    Sources (checked 2026-10-10): OSU "Nutritive value of feeds for beef cattle" (DM/CP/TDN of
    maize, wheat, barley); OSU "An introduction to finishing beef" and "Supplementing beef cows"
    (intake ranges); OSU wheat feeding fact sheet and 2025 Cow-Calf Corner (wheat limits,
    adaptation); Penn State "Total mixed rations for dairy cows" (dairy intake); NRC Beef 2000,
    NRC Dairy 2001 (book values for forages, NDF, starch, energy equations).

    Copy this file to modSettings/FS25_RealisticLivestockRM/feedProfiles.xml to change it; that
    copy replaces this file. A file that fails a check keeps the mode off and logs why; a single
    feed that fails is left uneaten and listed.
-->
<feedProfiles version="1" resourceScale="0.125" realDaysPerPeriod="30" stepDays="0.25">

    <requirements maintenanceMEPerKgMetabolic="0.50" growthMEPerKgAt500="50" growthWeightExponent="0.75"
        growthMEPerKgMin="18" reserveDepositMEPerKg="52" reserveMobilizeMEPerKg="38" catabolismMEPerKg="10"
        pregnancyFactor="0.35"/>

    <calf maintenanceMEPerKgMetabolic="0.42" negCoefficient="0.84" negWeightExponent="0.355" negGainExponent="1.2"
        kgMilk="0.69" kgSolid="0.57" minGainKgPerDay="0.2"/>

    <intake growingPerKgMetabolic="0.105" dryPerKgMetabolic="0.090" lactatingPerKgMetabolic="0.0968"
        lactatingPerKgMilk="0.372" calfSolidPerKgMetabolic="0.105"
        ndfFillGrowing="0.0125" ndfFillDry="0.0115" ndfFillLactating="0.0135" ndfFillCalf="0.0100"/>

    <health extremeUnderweightRatio="0.45" shortageBufferDays="3"/>

    <calfMilk realLitersPerDay="10" density="1.03" dm="0.125" sizeExponent="0.75"/>

    <damMilk dm="0.125" me="22.5" cp="0.25"/>

    <protein maintenance="0.065" calf="0.18" young="0.14" growing="0.12" finishing="0.105"
        lactating="0.16" dry="0.10" youngUntilMonth="8" growingUntilMonth="14" lactatingAtKg="30"/>

    <ration wheatMaxShare="0.30" adaptationDays="30" adaptationMaxShare="0.80" stepAllowance="0.20"
        unadaptedUtilisation="0.85">
        <class name="CALF" peNdfMin="0" fermentableMax="0.40"/>
        <class name="GROWING" peNdfMin="0.12" fermentableMax="0.55"/>
        <class name="LACTATING" peNdfMin="0.20" fermentableMax="0.35"/>
        <class name="DRY" peNdfMin="0.20" fermentableMax="0.25"/>
    </ration>

    <milk realKgPerDay="8" meMJPerKg="5.0">
        <breed name="HOLSTEIN" realKgPerDay="30"/>
        <breed name="REDHOLSTEIN_PACK" realKgPerDay="28"/>
        <breed name="SWISS_BROWN" realKgPerDay="25"/>
        <breed name="MONTBELIARDE" realKgPerDay="24"/>
        <breed name="AYRSHIRE_PACK" realKgPerDay="22"/>
        <breed name="SHORTHORNMILKERS_PACK" realKgPerDay="21"/>
        <breed name="JERSEY_PACK" realKgPerDay="20" meMJPerKg="6.0"/>
        <breed name="GUERNSEY_PACK" realKgPerDay="19" meMJPerKg="5.7"/>
        <breed name="VOSGIENNE" realKgPerDay="15"/>
        <breed name="KERRY_PACK" realKgPerDay="13"/>
        <breed name="WATER_BUFFALO" realKgPerDay="7" meMJPerKg="7.5"/>
        <breed name="SIMMENTAL_PACK" realKgPerDay="11"/>
        <breed name="CHAROLAIS_PACK" realKgPerDay="9"/>
        <breed name="SHORTHORN_PACK" realKgPerDay="9"/>
        <breed name="ANGUS" realKgPerDay="8"/>
        <breed name="REDANGUS_PACK" realKgPerDay="8"/>
        <breed name="IRISHMOILED_PACK" realKgPerDay="8"/>
        <breed name="HEREFORD" realKgPerDay="7"/>
        <breed name="LIMOUSIN" realKgPerDay="7"/>
        <breed name="BRITISHBLUE_PACK" realKgPerDay="7"/>
        <breed name="HIGHLAND" realKgPerDay="6"/>
        <breed name="BELTEDGALLOWAY_PACK" realKgPerDay="6"/>
    </milk>

    <!-- Complete rations: the game's TMR is mixed in the mixer wagon or by the feeding robot and
         no recipe is kept with it, so it is counted as one standard TMR (corn and grass silage, hay
         and grain), never as its parts. Not a measured ration. -->
    <feed fillType="FORAGE" role="COMPLETE" status="PROXY" form="TMR, standard proxy recipe"
        density="0.30" dm="0.45" tdn="72" meBasis="TDN_NRC2000" cp="0.16" ndf="0.36" peFactor="0.65"
        starch="0.22" sugar="0.03" stages="PRE_WEANING WEANING WEANED"
        source="Standard TMR proxy within NRC 2001 dairy TMR ranges; density = engine massPerLiter, dm per design doc" year="2001"/>
    <feed fillType="FORAGEPLUS" role="COMPLETE" status="PROXY" form="TMR with mineral feed (Hof Bergmann)"
        density="0.30" dm="0.45" tdn="73" meBasis="TDN_NRC2000" cp="0.17" ndf="0.36" peFactor="0.65"
        starch="0.22" sugar="0.03" stages="PRE_WEANING WEANING WEANED"
        source="As FORAGE with mineral feed; Hof Bergmann fillTypes" year="2001"/>
    <!-- ROUNDBALE (Hof Bergmann's cattle forage group) is a bale object's marker fill type (300 kg a
         liter), never a feed: a bale put into a trough adds its own content (hay, silage, straw:
         BaleUnloadTrigger, bale:getFillType), which these profiles value. Nothing on the map makes
         ROUNDBALE as bulk, so it never holds feed; kept unconfigured. -->
    <feed fillType="ROUNDBALE" role="COMPLETE" status="UNCONFIGURED" form="Hof Bergmann bale marker (massPerLiter 300), not a feed"
        source="A bale adds its content fill type to a trough" year="0"/>

    <!-- Forages -->
    <feed fillType="SILAGE" role="FORAGE" status="TYPICAL" form="grass / maize silage, chopped"
        density="0.45" dm="0.35" tdn="66" meBasis="TDN_NRC2000" cp="0.11" ndf="0.48" peFactor="0.85"
        starch="0.15" sugar="0.02" stages="PRE_WEANING WEANING WEANED"
        source="Mean of grass and maize silage, NRC 2000/2001 tables; density = engine massPerLiter" year="2001"/>
    <feed fillType="DRYGRASS_WINDROW" role="FORAGE" status="TYPICAL" form="grass hay, long"
        density="0.07" dm="0.85" tdn="56" meBasis="TDN_NRC2000" cp="0.11" ndf="0.63" peFactor="1.0"
        starch="0.02" sugar="0.05" stages="PRE_WEANING WEANING WEANED"
        source="Mid-quality grass hay, NRC 2000; density = engine massPerLiter, dm per design doc" year="2000"/>
    <feed fillType="GRASS_WINDROW" role="FORAGE" status="TYPICAL" form="fresh grass, cut or grazed"
        density="0.30" dm="0.20" tdn="64" meBasis="TDN_NRC2000" cp="0.16" ndf="0.52" peFactor="0.9"
        starch="0.02" sugar="0.10" stages="PRE_WEANING WEANING WEANED"
        source="Cool-season pasture, vegetative to mid, NRC 2000; density = engine massPerLiter" year="2000"/>
    <feed fillType="CLOVER_WINDROW" role="FORAGE" status="TYPICAL" form="fresh clover-grass"
        density="0.30" dm="0.18" tdn="64" meBasis="TDN_NRC2000" cp="0.20" ndf="0.40" peFactor="0.9"
        starch="0.02" sugar="0.08" stages="PRE_WEANING WEANING WEANED"
        source="Fresh clover / legume-grass, NRC 2000; density = engine massPerLiter" year="2000"/>

    <!-- Concentrates -->
    <feed fillType="MAIZE" role="CONCENTRATE" status="TYPICAL" form="maize grain, whole (fed as harvested)"
        density="0.80" dm="0.88" tdn="88" meBasis="TDN_NRC2000" cp="0.09" ndf="0.10" peFactor="0.4"
        starch="0.72" sugar="0.02" stages="WEANED"
        source="OSU Nutritive value of feeds (DM/CP/TDN); NDF/starch NRC 2001; density = engine massPerLiter" year="2026"/>
    <feed fillType="WHEAT" role="CONCENTRATE" status="TYPICAL" form="wheat grain (processing not tracked)"
        density="0.78" dm="0.89" tdn="84" meBasis="TDN_NRC2000" cp="0.14" ndf="0.12" peFactor="0.4"
        starch="0.64" sugar="0.02" stages="WEANED"
        source="OSU Nutritive value of feeds (DM/CP/TDN); NDF/starch NRC 2001; limit OSU 2025" year="2026"/>
    <feed fillType="BARLEY" role="CONCENTRATE" status="TYPICAL" form="dry barley grain"
        density="0.68" dm="0.90" tdn="81" meBasis="TDN_NRC2000" cp="0.12" ndf="0.20" peFactor="0.4"
        starch="0.56" sugar="0.02" stages="WEANED"
        source="OSU Nutritive value of feeds (DM/CP/TDN); NDF/starch NRC 2001" year="2026"/>
    <feed fillType="OAT" role="CONCENTRATE" status="TYPICAL" form="oat grain"
        density="0.50" dm="0.89" tdn="77" meBasis="TDN_NRC2000" cp="0.13" ndf="0.30" peFactor="0.4"
        starch="0.42" sugar="0.02" stages="WEANED"
        source="NRC 2000 book value" year="2000"/>
    <feed fillType="SUGARBEET_CUT" role="CONCENTRATE" status="TYPICAL" form="sugar beet, chopped"
        density="0.70" dm="0.23" tdn="80" meBasis="TDN_NRC2000" cp="0.07" ndf="0.12" peFactor="0.3"
        starch="0" sugar="0.65" stages="WEANED"
        source="Fodder/sugar beet roots, NRC 2000; density = engine massPerLiter" year="2000"/>
    <feed fillType="PEA" role="CONCENTRATE" status="TYPICAL" form="field pea grain, whole"
        density="0.80" dm="0.89" tdn="87" meBasis="TDN_NRC2000" cp="0.24" ndf="0.15" peFactor="0.4"
        starch="0.46" sugar="0.05" maxShare="0.40" stages="PRE_WEANING WEANING WEANED"
        source="NRC 2000 book value (TDN 87); CP/NDF/starch/sugar Feedipedia pea seeds; NDSU AS-1301 field peas up to 40% of growing diets and calf starters; density = Hof Bergmann massPerLiter (base game 0.72)" year="2000"/>
    <!-- Hof Bergmann's grain grist: its mills take wheat, barley, rye, oat and sorghum as one
         GRAINFRUITS (their identity is merged at the mill's unloading) and grind them 1:1 by liter,
         so what a load holds cannot be traced. A stated proxy recipe stands for it: 50% wheat and
         50% barley of the DM, each value the mean of the two profiles above. density 0.73 is the
         recipe's mean grain density (not the engine's 0.88): the 1:1 mill neither makes nor loses
         feed. Capped with wheat (capGroup): grist and wheat together at most wheat's 30% - it may be
         all wheat, and ground grain ferments faster. -->
    <feed fillType="GRAINGRIST" role="CONCENTRATE" status="PROXY" form="ground small grain; proxy recipe 50% wheat + 50% barley (DM)"
        density="0.73" dm="0.895" tdn="82.5" meBasis="TDN_NRC2000" cp="0.13" ndf="0.16" peFactor="0.3"
        starch="0.60" sugar="0.02" maxShare="0.30" capGroup="WHEAT" stages="WEANED"
        source="Proxy: mean of the WHEAT and BARLEY profiles (OSU / NRC 2001); Hof Bergmann grainMillSmall and contract mill GRAINFRUITS 1:1" year="2026"/>

    <!-- Milk: drunk by calves only (not rumen-fermented, so no starch or fibre counted). -->
    <feed fillType="CALFMILK" role="MILK" status="PROXY" form="milk replacer, 12.5% solids"
        density="1.03" dm="0.125" me="19.5" meBasis="ME_DIRECT" cp="0.22" ndf="0" peFactor="0"
        starch="0" sugar="0" stages="PRE_WEANING WEANING"
        source="NRC 2001 calf: milk replacer ME 4.65 Mcal/kg DM" year="2001"/>
</feedProfiles>
